From b80a9cdb2e7b5fb9e63d2bda635ee1e97625f6e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:00:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: tell of a held mode change with the commit
 that makes it

A change of mode that is held for the output of the video that opens
(CModeChangeHold, START) told the display resources that the display was
lost when the mode was chosen, and armed the reset from that moment. The
commit that applies the mode came later: with the first frame of native
Dolby Vision, or with the first ordinary commit after the renderer was
configured or the deadline passed. So OnResetDisplay() could reach
VideoPlayer and the audio engine before the display had changed at all,
and the change itself then came unannounced. The audio engine, which
with no sink waits for the reset while the display is lost, tried its
sink before the change and met the device changes of the change after
it.

Now the hold only chooses the mode. CHeldModeAnnouncement decides when
the change is told: before the first commit that carries it, of any
kind (the first frame of native output, a commit of the ordinary output
after the configure or the deadline, Hide() or Show()), with the reset
after it, armed with the delay that the user set, as SetFullScreen()
does. A frame of native output that tells of the change is not
presented a frame ahead: the loss flushes the renderer, so the GPU work
of that frame is finished first. When the driver refuses that frame the
mode did not change: the announcement is armed again for the next commit
that is not a frame of native output (the restoration of the ordinary
output, or a commit of it), never for the frames attempted every
refresh. A hold that a change of mode ends (SetFullScreen()) is not
told: that change tells of itself. A hold that cancels or expires is
told with whichever commit then makes the mode.

OnLostDevice() is told once until the display is back
(SendDisplayReset()): the resources hear of a loss and of its end in
pairs, never of a loss twice in a row (a change of mode before the reset
of the one before, or a held change told while a stalled flip turns the
output off and on). The reset timer is set again by each change, so the
reset still comes after the last one.

Every display resource that Kodi registers with this window system hears
of a change only through OnLostDevice() and OnResetDisplay()
(VideoPlayer, the audio engine, the video clock; VDPAU is X11 only), so
these two bracket the commit that reaches the display in every case.

Host model of the announcements per transition, with the old behaviour
as the counterexample: HDR10 start "lost, commit, reset"; native DV
start "lost, commit, reset, frames"; refused first frame "lost, refused
frame, reset, refused frame, lost, restoration, reset"; a hold that
expires "lost, commit, reset"; a hold dropped by a change of mode, told
only by that change. Five mutations of the decision (told every frame,
not armed again after a refusal, never told, kept after a drop, told
state never cleared) all make it fail.
